---
// TODO (Tim): skeleton in your voice, not your CV. Swap in the real specifics —
// stack, domains, years, the bits you want to be found for. Deliberately no
// invented employers or dates.
// Keep notes in this fence, not in HTML comments: Astro passes `<!-- -->`
// straight through to the rendered page.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Who I am, what I work on, and why this site leans towards write-ups rather than screenshots."
>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    I'm a software engineer. I've spent a long time shipping things, and rather less time
    writing about it than I probably should have. This site is the correction.
  </p>

  <h2>What I work on</h2>
  <p>
    Mostly backend and platform work, with a steady interest in the seam between
    engineering practice and tooling — the parts of delivery that quietly decide whether a
    team ships weekly or quarterly.
  </p>

  <h2>AI Ops</h2>
  <p>
    The other half of what I write about here. Not "AI will write your code", but the more
    interesting and less glamorous question: where do agents actually fit into an existing
    SDLC? Review, triage, migration, test scaffolding, documentation that stays current.
    What needs a human gate, what doesn't, and what breaks when you get that boundary
    wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'm sceptical by default and reasonably well-calibrated about it. Things that work get
    written up; so do the things that didn't.
  </p>

  <h2>What you won't find here</h2>
  <p>
    Any employer code. Everything on this site is concepts, patterns and write-ups —
    generalised deliberately, and written from my own projects where there's code to show.
  </p>

  <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a></li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
